Claude Sonnet thinks. Gemini models implement.
When a Gemini model reads this file, it must follow the roadmap below
step-by-step without skipping phases. All decisions were made here.
Gemini's job is precise, clean code — no architectural changes unless
explicitly listed as a step.

---

## PROJECT ORIENTATION (READ BEFORE ANY STEP)

### What the project is
A 2D evolutionary locomotion simulator. A quadruped creature ("pod pedal walker")
is physically simulated in Pymunk. A population of small neural networks (15->16->4,
Tanh activations) are evolved via a genetic algorithm (crossover + mutation) to
control its 4 leg motors. Training runs headlessly on multi-core CPU. Results
stream live to a Flask browser dashboard via Server-Sent Events (SSE).

### File map
  src/environment.py   - CreatureEnv: physics world, creature, terrain, reward
  src/evolution.py     - Population: evaluate, mutate, crossover, evolve
  src/model.py         - CreatureBrain: tiny NN controller
  src/utils.py         - Checkpoint helpers (atomic save, universal best)
  src/s3_sync.py       - Optional AWS S3 sync for checkpoints
  main.py              - CLI: --train / --render flags
  app.py               - Flask web server: SSE stream + REST training control
  templates/index.html - Browser dashboard (HTML5 Canvas + Chart.js)
  logs/history.csv     - Per-generation fitness metrics
  models/              - Saved .pth checkpoints

### Active constraints (MUST NOT VIOLATE)
- No heavy RL frameworks (no Ray, Stable-Baselines, PyTorch RL)
- No GPU dependency — CPU-only multiprocessing stays
- No aesthetic changes unless step explicitly says so
- No new Python packages unless step explicitly names them
- Always save models to models/, logs to logs/
- Fix bugs at root cause, never with try/except swallowing

---

## RESEARCH FINDINGS (CONTEXT FOR GEMINI)

### F1 — Terrain
Current terrain: 2 static Pymunk Segments (hardcoded uphill x:600-1600,
downhill x:1600-2600). This is too simple and fails to generalize creatures
beyond a single fixed profile. The field standard for 2D walker research
(2026) is continuous Perlin/simplex noise heightmaps sampled at regular
x-intervals, converted to chained pymunk.Segment objects, with steepness
increasing as a function of creature displacement.

### F2 — CPG Clock
Current CPG: fixed 30-step (sin, cos) lookup table. Step index modulo 30
drives the oscillation phase. Improvement: make CPG frequency a learnable
scalar (5th NN output: 4 motor rates + 1 frequency modifier). The frequency
modifier scales how fast the CPG clock advances. Creature adapts stride
rate to terrain slope automatically.

### F3 — NN Architecture
Current: 15 inputs -> Linear(16) -> Tanh -> Linear(4) -> Tanh
Improvement: 15 inputs -> Linear(32) -> Tanh -> Linear(16) -> Tanh -> Linear(4) -> Tanh
Params go from ~260 to ~660 — still tiny, still fast. Doubles hidden capacity
without breaking CPU constraint.

### F4 — Selection Strategy
Current: top-5 elitism by argsort, then random crossover from elites.
Improvement: tournament selection (k=3) replaces random elite sampling.
Picks 3 random individuals, takes the best, repeats for parent2. Preserves
diversity better than pure elitism.

### F5 — Training Speed
Current bottleneck: pool.map pickles entire state_dict for every genome.
SIMPLER fix (implement first): reduce max_steps from 600 to 200 for early
generations, scaling up to 600 by gen 67. Early creatures die fast anyway.

### F6 — SSE Stream
Current: every frame sends full render state JSON (~400 bytes) at 45fps.
Fix: send delta-state only (changed fields). Reduces bandwidth ~40-50%.
Also fix frame timing: proper elapsed-time accounting for true 45fps.

### F7 — Terrain in State Vector
Current state vector: 15 inputs, no terrain info. Creature is terrain-blind.
Fix: add 3 lookahead readings (ground_y at torso_x+50, +100, +150).
State vector grows: 15 -> 18 inputs. Model input_size must match.

